              "type": "text",
              "text": "<h2>Course Programme</h2><p><br></p><h3>Week 1: Your Money Story and Financial Wellbeing</h3><p>We will begin by exploring how family, culture, community and past experiences can shape our beliefs and feelings about money. You will consider how these influences may affect your financial habits and confidence today and learn practical ways to develop a healthier relationship with money.</p><p><strong><strong>By the end of this session, you will be able to:</strong></strong></p><ul><li>Identify money messages and beliefs you have absorbed throughout your life.</li><li>Recognise how your money mindset can influence your emotions, habits and financial decisions.</li><li>Reframe limiting beliefs about money in a more positive and constructive way.</li><li>Understand what financial wellbeing means and identify personal strengths that can help you build greater financial resilience.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Week 2: Taking Control of Your Money</h3><p>This practical session introduces cash flow management through five key money areas: income, expenses, tax, debt and savings. You will explore how these work together, consider your spending priorities and identify realistic ways to take greater control of your everyday finances.</p><p><strong><strong>By the end of this session, you will be able to:</strong></strong></p><ul><li>Understand cash flow and why it matters.</li><li>Recognise how income, expenses, tax, debt and savings work together.</li><li>Reflect on your spending patterns and financial priorities.</li><li>Create a realistic money plan suited to your circumstances.</li><li>Choose a manageable action you can take to improve your finances.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Week 3: Spending, Borrowing and Managing Debt</h3><p>Explore why we spend and how emotions, advertising, the brain’s reward system and fear of missing out can influence our choices. You will learn about the true cost of borrowing, credit reports and scores, different approaches to managing debt and reliable sources of support.</p><p><strong><strong>By the end of this session, you will be able to:</strong></strong></p><ul><li>Recognise some of the factors that influence spending decisions.</li><li>Identify the potential benefits, costs and risks of borrowing.</li><li>Understand how credit reports and scores work.</li><li>Recognise common approaches to repaying debt, including the snowball and avalanche methods.</li><li>Identify reliable sources of free guidance and support.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Week 4: Saving and Building Financial Resilience</h3><p>This session focuses on practical and achievable ways to build greater financial security. You will explore emergency funds, planning for irregular expenses, saving towards personal goals and organising savings according to different needs and timescales. You will also be introduced to common UK savings options, financial protection and fraud awareness.</p><p><strong><strong>By the end of this session, you will be able to:</strong></strong></p><ul><li>Understand the purpose of an emergency fund and choose an achievable starting target.</li><li>Organise savings around different goals and timescales.</li><li>Understand how interest and AER work and recognise common UK savings options.</li><li>Set realistic financial goals that reflect your priorities.</li><li>Spot common warning signs of financial fraud and scams.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Week 5: Introduction to Investing and Your Financial Future</h3><p>In the final session, you will explore the difference between saving and investing and be introduced to risk, compound growth and diversification. You will also learn about workplace and personal pensions, the State Pension, ISAs and common investment options, helping you identify appropriate next steps for your financial future.</p><p><strong><strong>By the end of this session, you will be able to:</strong></strong></p><ul><li>Distinguish between saving and investing and understand when each may be appropriate.</li><li>Understand investment risk and how returns can build over time.</li><li>Recognise common types of investments, including shares, bonds and funds.</li><li>Understand the basic roles of pensions and ISAs.</li><li>Identify realistic next steps towards your longer-term financial goals.</li></ul><h2><br></h2><p><br></p><h3><strong><strong>ABOUT THE TUTOR:</strong></strong></h3>",
